{
  "image_name": "e79494ccf51587a45e1621ccfc7b53ca.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how silicon–graphite battery anodes age through coupled chemical and mechanical degradation. At the electrode level, decomposition products clog pores, increase impedance, and reduce power, while gas generation swells the cell and displaces electrolyte. The drawing emphasizes that small Si particles have high reactive surface area, and that reactions involving Si, the binder, and electrolyte are especially harmful. A hydrolytic cycle builds up HF in the electrolyte, which appears central to the failure mechanism.\n\nThe enlarged schematic shows an aged Si particle with a Si core surrounded by an unstable SEI that continually changes and becomes mechanically destabilized. The SEI is depicted as having a porous organic outer layer and an inorganic inner layer. HF and water drive ongoing electrolyte decomposition, producing species such as fluorinated phosphate compounds and silicon fluorides. HF etches both SEI components and the Si active material, while soluble products can migrate and react at the cathode. In contrast, the graphite side appears relatively protected by a passivating SEI and is only minimally affected by HF. Overall, the figure conveys a self-reinforcing degradation loop centered on Si reactivity, SEI instability, and HF formation.",
